0:000 SekundenRight now, as you lie here listening to this, your brain is doing something it will not tell you about.
0:099 SekundenSomewhere behind your awareness, it is deciding which parts of today to keep and which parts to quietly dissolve.
0:1818 SekundenThe conversation you had this morning, the thing you read on your phone, the face of someone you passed on the street, most of it will be gone by tomorrow.
0:2929 SekundenNot because your memory is bad, but because your brain is selective in a way that is genuinely strange when you stop
0:3636 Sekundenand think about it. It keeps things you never meant to learn. Song lyrics from 15 years ago. The layout of a house you haven't visited since childhood.
0:4848 SekundenThe exact tone of voice someone used when they said something that hurt. And yet it lets go of things you actively deliberately tried to memorize.
0:5858 SekundenThe password you set last week, the name of the person you were just introduced to. The thing you studied for hours the
1:061 Minute, 6 Sekundennight before an exam and then could not retrieve when you sat down in front of the paper. That gap between what sticks
1:151 Minute, 15 Sekundenand what doesn't, between what you tried to learn and what you actually learned is one of the most confusing things
1:221 Minute, 22 Sekundenabout being a person. And tonight is about all of it. how humans learn. Not the inspirational version, not the
1:311 Minute, 31 Sekundenproductivity hack version, but the real version, which turns out to be stranger, messier, and more beautiful than almost
1:391 Minute, 39 Sekundenanyone expects. How a newborn goes from knowing almost nothing to understanding language in about 18 months, which is
1:481 Minute, 48 Sekundensomething no computer on Earth can properly replicate.
1:531 Minute, 53 Sekundenhow your brain physically changes shape when you practice something.
1:581 Minute, 58 SekundenWhy forgetting is not the opposite of learning but might actually be part of it. Why some things you learn once and
2:062 Minuten, 6 Sekundennever forget and other things you study over and over and they never quite take hold.
2:132 Minuten, 13 SekundenWhy the ways most people try to learn are according to decades of careful research almost exactly wrong.
2:222 Minuten, 22 SekundenSo settle in. There's nothing you need to remember from this. Nothing to take notes on. If you drift off somewhere in
2:302 Minuten, 30 Sekundenthe middle, that's fine. That's actually your brain doing one of the things we're going to talk about. Just let the ideas
2:382 Minuten, 38 Sekundenwash over you and whatever sticks sticks. The strange thing is that for most of human history, nobody really
2:472 Minuten, 47 Sekundenasked how learning works. People obviously knew that learning happened.
2:532 Minuten, 53 SekundenYou could watch a child go from helpless to competent. You could watch an apprentice become a master.
3:003 MinutenBut the question of what was actually going on inside, what changed in the mind or the body
3:063 Minuten, 6 Sekundenwhen someone learned something new, that question barely existed before a few curious people in the 1800s started poking at it.
3:163 Minuten, 16 SekundenThe first person who really tried to measure learning in a controlled way was a German psychologist named Herman Ebbinghouse. And his story is worth
3:263 Minuten, 26 Sekundentelling because it's so wonderfully obsessive.
3:293 Minuten, 29 SekundenIn the 1880s, Ebinghouse wanted to study pure learning. Learning stripped of meaning. Learning with no story attached to it, no emotion, no context.
3:433 Minuten, 43 SekundenHe wanted to see what the raw machinery of memory could do on its own. So, he invented a tool that sounds almost
3:503 Minuten, 50 Sekundencomically boring. He created lists of nonsense syllables, threeletter combinations that didn't mean anything.
3:583 Minuten, 58 SekundenThings like dax and bup and zol, hundreds of them. And then he sat down
4:044 Minuten, 4 Sekundenalone day after day and memorized these lists. He was both the researcher and
4:114 Minuten, 11 Sekundenthe only subject. He would learn a list, wait a specific amount of time, and then test himself to see how much he could recall.
4:214 Minuten, 21 SekundenWhat he found was the first real map of how memory behaves over time. And it's still one of the most reliable findings in all of psychology.
4:324 Minuten, 32 SekundenHe discovered that forgetting is not gradual and steady. It's steep and front-loaded.
4:404 Minuten, 40 SekundenYou lose most of what you learned almost immediately, within the first hour, and then the forgetting slows down dramatically.
4:494 Minuten, 49 SekundenThe stuff that survives that first steep drop tends to hang around for a long time. He drew this out as a curve, and
4:584 Minuten, 58 Sekundenit's been called the forgetting curve ever since.
5:025 Minuten, 2 SekundenAnd here's what's remarkable. When later researchers repeated his work with better methods, larger groups, and real
5:105 Minuten, 10 Sekundenwords instead of nonsense syllables, the basic shape of that curve held up. It's one of the most replicated findings in
5:195 Minuten, 19 Sekundenthe field. You forget fast, then slow, and the things that make it past that initial cliff are surprisingly durable.
5:285 Minuten, 28 SekundenBut Ebinghouse found something else. something that matters even more.
5:355 Minuten, 35 SekundenHe discovered that relearning something you've forgotten is faster than learning it the first time. Even when he couldn't
5:435 Minuten, 43 Sekundenconsciously recall a single syllable from a list when he sat down to learn that list again, it took fewer repetitions than it had originally.
5:545 Minuten, 54 SekundenSomething had been saved. Even though he couldn't access it, the memory wasn't gone. It was just unreachable.
6:036 Minuten, 3 SekundenAnd the act of relearning it carved the path a little deeper each time. Think about what that means for a second. It
6:116 Minuten, 11 Sekundenmeans that the feeling of having completely forgotten something, that blank, frustrated feeling of I used to
6:186 Minuten, 18 Sekundenknow this and now it's just gone. That feeling is often wrong. The trace is still in there. Your brain kept
6:276 Minuten, 27 Sekundensomething, some faint scaffolding even after the conscious memory faded. And if you come back to it, you'll find the
6:346 Minuten, 34 Sekundenrebuilding goes faster than the original construction.
6:386 Minuten, 38 SekundenEbinghouse was measuring this in the 1880s with nonsense syllables in a notebook. And the principle he uncovered
6:456 Minuten, 45 Sekundenis still the foundation of how we understand spaced repetition today. the idea that spreading your practice out
6:526 Minuten, 52 Sekundenover time with gaps in between is dramatically more effective than cramming everything into one session.
