very instinct you 1:13:061 Stunde, 13 Minuten, 6 Sekundenhave about what good studying is supposed to feel like. When you cram, you're reading the same material over 1:13:141 Stunde, 13 Minuten, 14 Sekundenand over in a short window. Each time you reread it, it feels more familiar. 1:13:201 Stunde, 13 Minuten, 20 SekundenThe sentences start to feel like old friends. You can almost predict what comes next on the page. And your brain 1:13:281 Stunde, 13 Minuten, 28 Sekundeninterprets that fluency, that smoothness as evidence that you've learned it. It feels like mastery. 1:13:361 Stunde, 13 Minuten, 36 SekundenBut what's actually happening is something much more shallow. You've become familiar with the surface of the 1:13:421 Stunde, 13 Minuten, 42 Sekundenmaterial, the way it looks, the rhythm of the sentences, the general shape of the ideas. You haven't built the kind of 1:13:511 Stunde, 13 Minuten, 51 Sekundendeep, flexible retrieval pathways that would let you pull the information up cold. Without the page in front of you 1:14:001 Stunde, 14 Minutenin a different context weeks from now, you've essentially confused recognition for recall. And those are very different 1:14:101 Stunde, 14 Minuten, 10 Sekundenthings. Recognition is easy. It's the feeling of seeing something and knowing you've encountered it before. 1:14:181 Stunde, 14 Minuten, 18 SekundenRecall is hard. It's the ability to generate the answer from scratch with no cues, no prompts, nothing to trigger it 1:14:271 Stunde, 14 Minuten, 27 Sekundenexcept the question itself. And the uncomfortable truth that Bjork's work keeps circling back to is that real 1:14:351 Stunde, 14 Minuten, 35 Sekundenlearning, the kind that lasts, the kind that transfers to new situations, requires recall. It requires the 1:14:441 Stunde, 14 Minuten, 44 Sekundeneffortful, sometimes frustrating act of pulling something out of memory rather than simply letting it wash over you again. 1:14:541 Stunde, 14 Minuten, 54 SekundenAnd the reason that effort matters so much has to do with how memory retrieval actually works at a mechanical level. 1:15:021 Stunde, 15 Minuten, 2 SekundenEvery time you successfully retrieve something from memory, you don't just access the trace, you strengthen it. The 1:15:101 Stunde, 15 Minuten, 10 Sekundenact of retrieval itself changes the memory, makes the pathway to it more robust, more accessible next time. 1:15:181 Stunde, 15 Minuten, 18 SekundenThis is sometimes called the testing effect, and it's one of the most reliable findings in all of cognitive psychology. 1:15:281 Stunde, 15 Minuten, 28 SekundenHenry Roiger and Jeffrey Carpick, working at Washington University in St. 1:15:331 Stunde, 15 Minuten, 33 SekundenLewis published a beautifully clean demonstration of this in 2006. 1:15:401 Stunde, 15 Minuten, 40 SekundenThey had students read short pros passages and then either restudy them or take a practice test on them, just 1:15:471 Stunde, 15 Minuten, 47 Sekundentrying to recall what they'd read with no feedback, no grades, nothing at stake. 1:15:551 Stunde, 15 Minuten, 55 Sekunden5 minutes later, the restudy group did slightly better on a final test. But a week later, the testing group 1:16:031 Stunde, 16 Minuten, 3 Sekundendramatically outperformed the restudy group. 1:16:071 Stunde, 16 Minuten, 7 SekundenThe students who had practiced retrieving the material remembered far more of it than the students who had spent the same amount of time simply 1:16:141 Stunde, 16 Minuten, 14 Sekundenreading it again. Think about what that means. 1:16:191 Stunde, 16 Minuten, 19 SekundenThe students who felt like they were studying less effectively, who sat there struggling to remember details and 1:16:261 Stunde, 16 Minuten, 26 Sekundenprobably felt frustrated by how much they'd already forgotten, actually learned more. 1:16:321 Stunde, 16 Minuten, 32 SekundenAnd the students who spent their time calmly rereading, feeling the material become more and more familiar, feeling increasingly confident, retained less. 1:16:451 Stunde, 16 Minuten, 45 SekundenThe feeling of learning and the fact of learning were pointing in opposite directions. 1:16:501 Stunde, 16 Minuten, 50 SekundenThis is the heart of what Bjork means by desirable difficulties. The difficulty is desirable precisely because it forces 1:16:581 Stunde, 16 Minuten, 58 Sekundenthe kind of processing that builds durable memory and retrieval practice is just one example. There are others and 1:17:061 Stunde, 17 Minuten, 6 Sekundenthey all share the same unsettling signature. They all feel worse while working better. 1:17:131 Stunde, 17 Minuten, 13 SekundenSpacing is probably the most powerful of them. Instead of studying something in one concentrated 1:17:201 Stunde, 17 Minuten, 20 Sekundenblock, you spread your practice out over time with gaps in between. And during those 1:17:281 Stunde, 17 Minuten, 28 Sekundengaps, you forget some of what you learned. That's the whole point. When you come back to the material after a 1:17:361 Stunde, 17 Minuten, 36 Sekundendelay, you have to work harder to retrieve it. And that harder retrieval strengthens the memory more than an easy one would have. 1:17:461 Stunde, 17 Minuten, 46 SekundenThe spacing effect, as it's called, was actually one of the first things Ebinghouse noticed all the way back in the 1880s when he was memorizing those 1:17:551 Stunde, 17 Minuten, 55 Sekundennonsense syllables and tracking his own forgetting curves. 1:18:001 Stunde, 18 MinutenHe found that distributed practice produced better retention than mass practice. But for over a century, 1:18:071 Stunde, 18 Minuten, 7 Sekundeneducation mostly ignored this. The structure of schools with their unit tests and semester exams and the 1:18:151 Stunde, 18 Minuten, 15 Sekundenassumption that you cover a topic, test it, and move on is almost perfectly designed to produce mass practice and rapid forgetting. 1:18:261 Stunde, 18 Minuten, 26 SekundenBork has pointed this out many times with a kind of patient, beused frustration that you can hear in his lectures if you ever look them up. 1:18:351 Stunde, 18 Minuten, 35 SekundenAnd then there's interle which might be the strangest of these desirable difficulties. The idea is simple. 1:18:441 Stunde, 18 Minuten, 44 SekundenInstead of practicing one type of problem over and over before moving to the next type, you mix them up. 1:18:521 Stunde, 18 Minuten, 52 SekundenIf you're learning to identify different species of birds or different painting styles or different types of math problems, you don't do all the examples 1:19:011 Stunde, 19 Minuten, 1 Sekundeof one category and then all the examples of the next. You shuffle them together. And this feels terrible. It 1:19:081 Stunde, 19 Minuten, 8 Sekundenfeels confusing and slow and like you're not making progress. 1:19:141 Stunde, 19 Minuten, 14 SekundenWhen you're doing blocked practice, doing all the same type in a row, you quickly get into a groove. You figure 1:19:221 Stunde, 19 Minuten, 22 Sekundenout the pattern and you start getting them right and it feels productive. 1:19:281 Stunde, 19 Minuten, 28 SekundenWhen you interle, you keep having to switch strategies. You make more errors and the whole experience feels messier. 1:19:371 Stunde, 19 Minuten, 37 SekundenBut the research, much of it done by Borc and his wife Elizabeth Bjork, who is also a psychologist and has been his 1:19:441 Stunde, 19 Minuten, 44 Sekundencollaborator for decades, shows that interle produces better learning in the long run. Specifically, better ability 1:19:531 Stunde, 19 Minuten, 53 Sekundento discriminate between categories and to apply the right strategy to a new problem. 1:20:001 Stunde, 20 MinutenThe reason seems to be that interle forces you to practice the act of choosing which approach to use, not just 1:20:081 Stunde, 20 Minuten, 8 Sekundenexecuting the approach once you've been told which one it is. And in real life outside the classroom, nobody tells you 1:20:161 Stunde, 20 Minuten, 16 Sekundenwhich type of problem you're facing. You have to figure that out yourself. So interle practices the whole skill, not 1:20:261 Stunde, 20 Minuten, 26 Sekundenjust the execution part. There's a study by Doug Roer and Kelly Taylor from 2007 that illustrates this nicely. They had 1:20:351 Stunde, 20 Minuten, 35 Sekundenstudents learn to calculate the volumes of different geometric solids, four different types. 1:20:431 Stunde, 20 Minuten, 43 SekundenHalf the students practiced in blocks, all the problems of one type, then all the problems of the next. The other half 1:20:521 Stunde, 20 Minuten, 52 Sekundenpracticed the same problems interled, all mixed together. 1:20:571 Stunde, 20 Minuten, 57 SekundenDuring practice, the blocked group got about 90% correct. The interled group 1:21:041 Stunde, 21 Minuten, 4 Sekundengot only about 60% correct. It looked like blocking was clearly superior. But on a surprise test a week later, the 1:21:131 Stunde, 21 Minuten, 13 Sekundenpattern flipped completely. The blocked group got about 20% correct. The interled group got about 63% correct. 1:21:231 Stunde, 21 Minuten, 23 SekundenThe students who had struggled more during practice retained more than three times as much. And you've almost 1:21:301 Stunde, 21 Minuten, 30 Sekundencertainly felt this yourself, even if you didn't have a name for it. You've had the experience of studying something 1:21:391 Stunde, 21 Minuten, 39 Sekundenand feeling like you really knew it and then being surprised when it slipped away. And you've had the opposite 1:21:461 Stunde, 21 Minuten, 46 Sekundenexperience, too, of struggling with something, feeling like it wasn't clicking, and then finding weeks later 1:21:531 Stunde, 21 Minuten, 53 Sekundenthat it had somehow stuck. Those weren't random. Those were the desirable difficulties doing their work or their absence leaving a gap. 1:22:041 Stunde, 22 Minuten, 4 SekundenNow, there's an important qualification here that Bjork himself is careful about. Not all difficulties are desirable. A difficulty is only 1:22:141 Stunde, 22 Minuten, 14 Sekundendesirable if it triggers the right kind of processing, the kind that strengthens retrieval and builds flexible understanding. 1:22:221 Stunde, 22 Minuten, 22 SekundenIf a difficulty is just confusing, if the material is poorly explained or the task is so hard that you can't even 1:22:291 Stunde, 22 Minuten, 29 Sekundenbegin to engage with it, that's not desirable. 1:22:331 Stunde, 22 Minuten, 33 SekundenThat's just hard. And Bork connects this back to something that should sound familiar by now. The idea of the zone of 1:22:411 Stunde, 22 Minuten, 41 Sekundenproximal development that Vigotssky described a desirable difficulty operates in that zone just beyond what's 1:22:501 Stunde, 22 Minuten, 50 Sekundencomfortable but within reach if you stretch. 1:22:551 Stunde, 22 Minuten, 55 SekundenToo easy and you don't learn. Too hard and you can't learn. The sweet spot is 1:23:021 Stunde, 23 Minuten, 2 Sekundenthe place where you have to work for it, but the work is productive. 1:23:071 Stunde, 23 Minuten, 7 SekundenAnd here's what's genuinely interesting about all of this from a broader perspective. 1:23:121 Stunde, 23 Minuten, 12 SekundenThese findings, the testing effect, spacing, interle, they're not controversial. 1:23:201 Stunde, 23 Minuten, 20 SekundenThey replicate beautifully. They work across ages, across domains, across cultures. Roiger has called the testing 1:23:291 Stunde, 23 Minuten, 29 Sekundeneffect one of the most robust findings in all of experimental psychology. 1:23:341 Stunde, 23 Minuten, 34 SekundenAnd yet, and yet most people don't use them. Most students reread their notes. 1:23:411 Stunde, 23 Minuten, 41 SekundenMost teachers assign blocked practice. 1:23:441 Stunde, 23 Minuten, 44 SekundenMost of us, when we want to learn something, do the thing that feels most effective rather than the thing that is 1:23:531 Stunde, 23 Minuten, 53 Sekundenmost effective. There's a deep irony here that Bork has written about extensively. 1:24:011 Stunde, 24 Minuten, 1 SekundeThe human mind is a spectacular learning machine, but it is a poor judge of its own learning. The metacognitive signals, 1:24:101 Stunde, 24 Minuten, 10 Sekundenthe feelings of fluency and confidence that tell you whether you're learning, are systematically miscalibrated. 1:24:181 Stunde, 24 Minuten, 18 SekundenThey track the ease of processing, not the depth of encoding. 1:24:231 Stunde, 24 Minuten, 23 SekundenThis connects to a broader phenomenon that the psychologist Nate Cornell, who was one of Bork's students, has studied in detail. 1:24:331 Stunde, 24 Minuten, 33 SekundenCornell found that even after people are shown the data, even after they experience the spacing effect firsthand 1:24:411 Stunde, 24 Minuten, 41 Sekundenand see that they retained more with spaced practice, many of them still report that masked practice felt more effective. 1:24:491 Stunde, 24 Minuten, 49 SekundenThe illusion is remarkably resistant to correction. 1:24:541 Stunde, 24 Minuten, 54 SekundenYour gut keeps telling you that fluency means learning even when you have direct evidence that it doesn't. And if you 1:25:021 Stunde, 25 Minuten, 2 Sekundenthink about it, this makes a kind of evolutionary sense. 1:25:061 Stunde, 25 Minuten, 6 SekundenFor most of the problems your ancestors faced, fluency was a reasonable proxy for mastery. If you could do something 1:25:141 Stunde, 25 Minuten, 14 Sekundensmoothly and easily, you probably had learned it. The kinds of abstract declarative knowledge that modern 1:25:221 Stunde, 25 Minuten, 22 Sekundeneducation asks you to retain, facts and theories and procedures disconnected from immediate physical practice are evolutionarily novel. 1:25:321 Stunde, 25 Minuten, 32 SekundenYour brain didn't evolve to cram for organic chemistry. 1:25:371 Stunde, 25 Minuten, 37 SekundenIt evolved to learn the terrain, to learn which plants were safe, to learn the social dynamics of a small group. 1:25:461 Stunde, 25 Minuten, 46 SekundenAnd for those kinds of learning, the feeling of ease and the fact of mastery 1:25:521 Stunde, 25 Minuten, 52 Sekundenprobably did line up pretty well. It's only when we started asking the brain to do something it wasn't designed for, to 1:26:001 Stunde, 26 Minutenmemorize arbitrary information on a schedule set by someone else that the gap between feeling and fact opened up. 1:26:111 Stunde, 26 Minuten, 11 SekundenWhich brings up something that sits underneath all of these findings about how learning works. Something that the science has been circling for decades without quite settling. 1:26:221 Stunde, 26 Minuten, 22 SekundenAnd that's the question of what learning is actually for. Not in the biological sense, not survival and reproduction, 1:26:301 Stunde, 26 Minuten, 30 Sekundenbut in the deeper sense of what the mind is trying to build when it learns. 1:26:361 Stunde, 26 Minuten, 36 SekundenBecause the desirable difficulties research suggests something surprising. 1:26:421 Stunde, 26 Minuten, 42 SekundenIt suggests that the mind isn't trying to record information. 1:26:461 Stunde, 26 Minuten, 46 SekundenIt's trying to build the ability to retrieve and use information in new contexts. 1:26:531 Stunde, 26 Minuten, 53 SekundenThe goal isn't storage. The goal is flexible access. And that distinction 1:27:001 Stunde, 27 Minutenbetween a mind that stores and a mind that constructs turns out to be at the center of one of the most important 1:27:071 Stunde, 27 Minuten, 7 Sekundenshifts in how we think about learning. A shift that's still playing out right now in classrooms and laboratories and in 1:27:171 Stunde, 27 Minuten, 17 Sekundenthe quiet ongoing work of your own mind as it makes sense of the world. 1:27:231 Stunde, 27 Minuten, 23 SekundenThat phrase, a mind that constructs, isn't a metaphor. 1:27:281 Stunde, 27 Minuten, 28 Sekundenor rather it started as a metaphor and then the evidence caught up with it and it turned out to be closer to a literal 1:27:351 Stunde, 27 Minuten, 35 Sekundendescription than anyone expected. The idea has a name, constructivism. 1:27:421 Stunde, 27 Minuten, 42 SekundenAnd it's one of those words that sounds like it belongs in a philosophy seminar, but it describes something so ordinary, 1:27:501 Stunde, 27 Minuten, 50 Sekundenso close to the texture of everyday thinking that once you see it, you can't quite unsee it. 1:27:571 Stunde, 27 Minuten, 57 SekundenHere's the simplest version. When you learn something, you don't copy it into your head the way you'd copy a file onto 1:28:051 Stunde, 28 Minuten, 5 Sekundena hard drive. You build a version of it out of materials you already have. 1:28:101 Stunde, 28 Minuten, 10 SekundenEverything you encounter, every new fact, every new idea, every new experience gets woven into the structure 1:28:191 Stunde, 28 Minuten, 19 Sekundenthat's already there. And in the process, both the new thing and the old 1:28:261 Stunde, 28 Minuten, 26 Sekundenstructure change a little. Learning isn't addition, it's renovation. You're not adding a room to a house. You're rearranging the walls. 1:28:361 Stunde, 28 Minuten, 36 SekundenJean Pia, who we spent time with earlier, the Swiss psychologist who watched children so carefully that he 1:28:441 Stunde, 28 Minuten, 44 Sekundenessentially invented the modern study of cognitive development. He was the one who laid the groundwork for this. 1:28:521 Stunde, 28 Minuten, 52 SekundenHe used two terms that sound technical but are actually beautifully simple. 1:28:581 Stunde, 28 Minuten, 58 SekundenAssimilation and accommodation. 1:29:021 Stunde, 29 Minuten, 2 SekundenAssimilation is what happens when you encounter something new and it fits neatly into a framework you already have. A child who knows what a dog is 1:29:111 Stunde, 29 Minuten, 11 Sekundensees a new dog and thinks dog easy. The new information slots right in. 1:29:191 Stunde, 29 Minuten, 19 SekundenAccommodation is what happens when something new doesn't fit. When the existing framework has to stretch or break to make room. The child sees a cat 1:29:291 Stunde, 29 Minuten, 29 Sekundenfor the first time and calls it a dog because it's small and furry and has four legs. And then someone says, "No, 1:29:371 Stunde, 29 Minuten, 37 Sekundenthat's a cat." And the child's internal category system has to reorganize. A new slot has to be created. The old slot, 1:29:471 Stunde, 29 Minuten, 47 Sekundensmall furry thing with four legs has to be refined. That's accommodation. And Pia argued that all learning from 1:29:561 Stunde, 29 Minuten, 56 Sekundeninfancy to adulthood is this constant back and forth between fitting new things into old frameworks and 1:30:031 Stunde, 30 Minuten, 3 Sekundenrebuilding old frameworks to handle new things. 1:30:081 Stunde, 30 Minuten, 8 SekundenNow PG was talking about children mostly but the constructivist idea didn't stay in developmental psychology. 1:30:161 Stunde, 30 Minuten, 16 SekundenIt migrated and the person who carried it furthest into the territory of education was a psychologist and 1:30:241 Stunde, 30 Minuten, 24 Sekundenphilosopher named Ernst von Glozersfeld an Austrian-born thinker who spent most of his career in the United States and 1:30:321 Stunde, 30 Minuten, 32 Sekundenwho in the 1980s and '90s pushed constructivism into what he called its 1:30:391 Stunde, 30 Minuten, 39 Sekundenradical form. And the radical part is this. Von Glazersfeld argued that we 1:30:461 Stunde, 30 Minuten, 46 Sekundennever have direct access to reality as it actually is. All we ever have is the models we've built from our experience. 1:30:551 Stunde, 30 Minuten, 55 SekundenKnowledge isn't a mirror of the world. 1:30:581 Stunde, 30 Minuten, 58 SekundenIt's a set of tools that have worked so far. When you learn something, you're not getting closer to the truth in some 1:31:051 Stunde, 31 Minuten, 5 Sekundenabsolute sense. You're building a model that's useful, that lets you navigate, predict, and act. And when that model 1:31:141 Stunde, 31 Minuten, 14 Sekundenstops working, when you hit something it can't handle, you rebuild. 1:31:201 Stunde, 31 Minuten, 20 SekundenThis sounds abstract, but think about what it means for something as concrete as learning physics. 1:31:271 Stunde, 31 Minuten, 27 SekundenA student walks into a physics class with a perfectly functional model of how the world works. Heavy things fall 1:31:351 Stunde, 31 Minuten, 35 Sekundenfaster than light things. That's what it looks like. That's what experience seems to show. 1:31:421 Stunde, 31 Minuten, 42 SekundenAnd then the teacher says, "Actually, in a vacuum, a feather and a bowling ball hit the ground at the same time." The 1:31:521 Stunde, 31 Minuten, 52 Sekundenstudent doesn't just add this fact to a mental filing cabinet. The student has to dismantle an existing model, one that 1:31:591 Stunde, 31 Minuten, 59 Sekundenhas worked fine for navigating everyday life, and build a new one. And that process is uncomfortable. 1:32:081 Stunde, 32 Minuten, 8 SekundenIt involves a period where the old model is broken and the new one isn't solid yet. 1:32:141 Stunde, 32 Minuten, 14 SekundenPJ had a word for that uncomfortable middle state too. He called it disequilibrium 1:32:211 Stunde, 32 Minuten, 21 Sekundenand he thought it was essential. Without it, without that wobble, the mind has no reason to rebuild. You can feel how this connects back to desirable difficulties. 1:32:341 Stunde, 32 Minuten, 34 SekundenThe Bjorks, Robert and Elizabeth, showed that learning that feels hard often produces better long-term retention and 1:32:421 Stunde, 32 Minuten, 42 Sekundentransfer. Constructivism offers a reason why. If learning is construction, then the effort of building is the learning. 1:32:511 Stunde, 32 Minuten, 51 SekundenThe struggle isn't an obstacle to understanding. It's the mechanism of understanding. 1:32:581 Stunde, 32 Minuten, 58 SekundenWhen you space your practice or test yourself instead of rereading or mix up different types of problems, you're 1:33:061 Stunde, 33 Minuten, 6 Sekundenforcing the mind to actively reconstruct, to pull apart and reassemble rather than passively recognize. 1:33:161 Stunde, 33 Minuten, 16 SekundenAnd that reconstruction is what strengthens the structure. But here's where constructivism gets genuinely 1:33:221 Stunde, 33 Minuten, 22 Sekundeninteresting, and where it starts to challenge some very deep assumptions about what a teacher is, what a 1:33:301 Stunde, 33 Minuten, 30 Sekundenclassroom is, and what it means to know something. If knowledge is constructed by the learner rather than transmitted 1:33:381 Stunde, 33 Minuten, 38 Sekundenby the teacher, then teaching isn't delivery. 1:33:421 Stunde, 33 Minuten, 42 SekundenYou can't pour understanding into someone's head. All you can do is create the conditions under which they're 1:33:491 Stunde, 33 Minuten, 49 Sekundenlikely to build it themselves. This might sound obvious when you say it out loud, but it runs against the grain of 1:33:571 Stunde, 33 Minuten, 57 Sekundenhow most education has been organized for centuries. The lecture, the textbook, the exam that asks you to reproduce what was said in the lecture. 1:34:091 Stunde, 34 Minuten, 9 SekundenAll of that assumes a transmission model. The teacher has the knowledge. 1:34:141 Stunde, 34 Minuten, 14 SekundenThe student receives the knowledge. The exam checks whether the transfer was successful. Constructivism says that 1:34:221 Stunde, 34 Minuten, 22 Sekundenmodel is fundamentally wrong, not slightly wrong, architecturally wrong. 1:34:301 Stunde, 34 Minuten, 30 SekundenAnd this is where the research gets complicated because constructivism as a philosophy of learning is one thing and 1:34:371 Stunde, 34 Minuten, 37 Sekundenconstructivism as a teaching method is another. And the two have not always been carefully separated. 1:34:441 Stunde, 34 Minuten, 44 SekundenIn the 1990s and 2000s, a wave of educational reform swept through many countries under broadly constructivist 1:34:521 Stunde, 34 Minuten, 52 Sekundenbanners. Discovery learning, inquirybased learning, problembased learning. 1:35:001 Stunde, 35 MinutenThe idea was that students should explore, investigate, and construct their own understanding with minimal direct instruction. 1:35:081 Stunde, 35 Minuten, 8 SekundenAnd the results were honestly mixed, sometimes spectacularly mixed. 1:35:151 Stunde, 35 Minuten, 15 SekundenA landmark paper published in 2006 by Paul Kersner, John Sweller, and Richard Clark argued that minimally guided 1:35:231 Stunde, 35 Minuten, 23 Sekundeninstruction, the kind of pure discovery learning that some constructivists championed, often fails and fails 1:35:311 Stunde, 35 Minuten, 31 Sekundenprecisely because it ignores what we know about how working memory works. 1:35:361 Stunde, 35 Minuten, 36 SekundenSweller, who had been developing something called cognitive load theory since the 1980s, showed that working 1:35:431 Stunde, 35 Minuten, 43 Sekundenmemory, that narrow bottleneck where you hold and manipulate new information, is severely limited. You can hold maybe four things in working memory at once. 1:35:541 Stunde, 35 Minuten, 54 SekundenAnd if a learning task overwhelms that capacity, learning doesn't just slow down, it stops. 1:36:021 Stunde, 36 Minuten, 2 SekundenAsking a novice to discover the principles of algebra by exploring problems on their own without guidance 1:36:091 Stunde, 36 Minuten, 9 Sekundencan overload working memory so badly that the student learns nothing at all or worse learns the wrong thing and 1:36:171 Stunde, 36 Minuten, 17 Sekundenbuilds a faulty model that's then harder to fix than if they'd started from scratch. 1:36:231 Stunde, 36 Minuten, 23 SekundenSo here's the tension. Constructivism says the learner has to build understanding themselves. 1:36:311 Stunde, 36 Minuten, 31 SekundenCognitive load theory says the learner's building capacity is extremely limited and can easily be overwhelmed. 1:36:391 Stunde, 36 Minuten, 39 SekundenBoth of these are well supported. Both are in their own domains about as solid as educational psychology gets. And the 1:36:481 Stunde, 36 Minuten, 48 Sekundenresolution, the place where the field has more or less settled, at least for now, is that the amount of guidance a 1:36:561 Stunde, 36 Minuten, 56 Sekundenlearner needs depends on how much they already know. 1:37:011 Stunde, 37 Minuten, 1 SekundeThis is sometimes called the expertise reversal effect and it's one of the most practically useful findings in all of learning science. 1:37:101 Stunde, 37 Minuten, 10 SekundenFor a novice, detailed, explicit instruction works better than discovery. 1:37:171 Stunde, 37 Minuten, 17 SekundenThe novice doesn't have enough existing structure to build on. So, they need scaffolding. And there's Bruner's word 1:37:241 Stunde, 37 Minuten, 24 Sekundenagain showing up decades later in a completely different context. But for someone who already has a solid 1:37:311 Stunde, 37 Minuten, 31 Sekundenframework, too much instruction actually gets in the way. It becomes redundant. 1:37:381 Stunde, 37 Minuten, 38 SekundenIt clutters working memory with things the learner could have generated on their own. And it prevents the kind of active reconstruction that deepens understanding. 1:37:481 Stunde, 37 Minuten, 48 SekundenThink about what that means. The best way to teach someone depends on what they already know, which depends on what 1:37:551 Stunde, 37 Minuten, 55 Sekundenthey've already built, which is different for every learner, which means there is no single best way to teach. 1:38:031 Stunde, 38 Minuten, 3 SekundenThere's only the right way for this person at this stage with this material. And that's a genuinely hard problem. 1:38:111 Stunde, 38 Minuten, 11 SekundenIt's the kind of problem that makes you understand why teaching well is so difficult and why simple prescriptions 1:38:191 Stunde, 38 Minuten, 19 Sekundenalways lecture or never lecture always turn out to be wrong. And you've felt this yourself even if you've never 1:38:281 Stunde, 38 Minuten, 28 Sekundenthought about it in these terms. You've had the experience of someone explaining something to you step by step carefully 1:38:351 Stunde, 38 Minuten, 35 Sekundenand clearly. and you followed every step and at the end you understood nothing. 1:38:421 Stunde, 38 Minuten, 42 SekundenEach piece made sense in isolation, but the whole thing didn't click and you've had the opposite experience where you 1:38:501 Stunde, 38 Minuten, 50 Sekundenstruggled with something on your own, got frustrated, maybe even gave up for a while. And then suddenly, sometimes in 1:38:591 Stunde, 38 Minuten, 59 Sekundenthe shower, sometimes lying in bed, the pieces fell into place and you understood it in a way that felt like it 1:39:061 Stunde, 39 Minuten, 6 Sekundenwas yours, not borrowed. That second experience, the one that feels like building, that's construction. 1:39:161 Stunde, 39 Minuten, 16 SekundenAnd the first experience, the one where everything made sense but nothing stuck. 1:39:211 Stunde, 39 Minuten, 21 SekundenThat's what happens when you receive a structure instead of building one. It looks like learning from the outside. It 1:39:311 Stunde, 39 Minuten, 31 Sekundenfeels like learning while it's happening, but it doesn't hold. This connects to something that a cognitive 1:39:391 Stunde, 39 Minuten, 39 Sekundenscientist named Susan Ambrose and her colleagues articulated in a book published in 2010, drawing together 1:39:471 Stunde, 39 Minuten, 47 Sekundendecades of research into a set of principles. One of their central points was that learning is not something done 1:39:551 Stunde, 39 Minuten, 55 Sekundento students. It's something students do, which sounds like a platitude until you 1:40:021 Stunde, 40 Minuten, 2 Sekundenrealize how much of the machinery of education is built on the opposite assumption. 1:40:081 Stunde, 40 Minuten, 8 Sekundenthe syllabus, the standardized test, the timed lecture, the coverage model that 1:40:151 Stunde, 40 Minuten, 15 Sekundentreats a subject as a territory to be marched across rather than a landscape to be explored. 1:40:221 Stunde, 40 Minuten, 22 SekundenAll of it assumes that if the teacher covers the material, the student has been exposed to it. And exposure should produce learning. 1:40:331 Stunde, 40 Minuten, 33 SekundenBut exposure doesn't produce learning. 1:40:351 Stunde, 40 Minuten, 35 SekundenConstruction produces learning and construction takes time and effort and the kind of productive confusion that 1:40:441 Stunde, 40 Minuten, 44 Sekundenmost educational systems are designed to minimize. 1:40:491 Stunde, 40 Minuten, 49 SekundenWhich brings us to something that researchers have been paying more and more attention to in recent years. 1:40:551 Stunde, 40 Minuten, 55 SekundenSomething that connects the constructivist insight to the neuroscience of how the brain actually changes when you learn. Because if 1:41:041 Stunde, 41 Minuten, 4 Sekundenlearning is construction, then there should be a physical signature of that construction in the brain. And there is. 1:41:131 Stunde, 41 Minuten, 13 SekundenIt's called neuroplasticity. 1:41:161 Stunde, 41 Minuten, 16 SekundenAnd it's a word you've probably heard, but the reality of it is stranger and more specific than the word usually implies. 1:41:251 Stunde, 41 Minuten, 25 SekundenWhen you learn something, your brain physically changes. Not metaphorically, not in some vague handwavy sense. 1:41:351 Stunde, 41 Minuten, 35 SekundenActual structural changes occur at the level of individual synapses. The tiny gaps between neurons where chemical 1:41:421 Stunde, 41 Minuten, 42 Sekundensignals pass. Connections that are used together get strengthened. Connections that aren't used get pruned. New connections form. 1:41:541 Stunde, 41 Minuten, 54 SekundenAnd this process, this constant remodeling doesn't stop when you're a child. It continues throughout your 1:42:021 Stunde, 42 Minuten, 2 Sekundenentire life. Though it slows down and the nature of it changes in ways that are worth understanding, 1:42:101 Stunde, 42 Minuten, 10 Sekundenespecially right now as your own brain is quietly doing some of this work while you lie here. 1:42:181 Stunde, 42 Minuten, 18 SekundenFor a long time, the assumption was that the brain was essentially finished by early adulthood. You got your neural 1:42:261 Stunde, 42 Minuten, 26 Sekundenarchitecture. Your connections solidified. And after that, you were more or less working with what you had. 1:42:341 Stunde, 42 Minuten, 34 SekundenThe idea had a certain intuitive appeal. 1:42:381 Stunde, 42 Minuten, 38 SekundenChildren learn languages effortlessly, pick up instruments faster, absorb new environments like water into a sponge. 1:42:471 Stunde, 42 Minuten, 47 SekundenAdults struggle with all of those things. So, it seemed reasonable to think that the brain's window for serious change closed sometime around 1:42:551 Stunde, 42 Minuten, 55 Sekundenthe end of adolescence, maybe the early 20s. And after that, you were in maintenance mode. 1:43:031 Stunde, 43 Minuten, 3 SekundenBut that turned out to be wrong. And the way we discovered it was wrong is itself a good story about how science corrects 1:43:111 Stunde, 43 Minuten, 11 Sekundenitself slowly, sometimes painfully, and often against the wishes of the people who built their careers on the earlier version. 1:43:201 Stunde, 43 Minuten, 20 SekundenThe turning point came in stages. One of the most striking pieces of evidence arrived in the late 1990s from a study 1:43:281 Stunde, 43 Minuten, 28 Sekundenby Elellanar Maguire and her colleagues at University College London. 1:43:331 Stunde, 43 Minuten, 33 SekundenThey looked at the brains of London taxi drivers, people who had spent years memorizing the city's impossibly tangled 1:43:411 Stunde, 43 Minuten, 41 Sekundenstreet layout through a process called the knowledge, which typically takes 2 to four years of intensive study. What 1:43:501 Stunde, 43 Minuten, 50 SekundenMaguire found using structural brain imaging was that the posterior hippocampus, the part of the brain 1:43:581 Stunde, 43 Minuten, 58 Sekundeninvolved in spatial memory and navigation, was physically larger in taxi drivers than in matched controls. 1:44:051 Stunde, 44 Minuten, 5 SekundenAnd the longer someone had been driving a taxi, the larger that region tended to be. This wasn't a case of people with 1:44:131 Stunde, 44 Minuten, 13 Sekundennaturally big hippocampy choosing to become taxi drivers. Follow-up work tracked trainees over time and found 1:44:221 Stunde, 44 Minuten, 22 Sekundenthat the hippocample changes developed during the training itself. The brain was literally reshaping in response to what was being learned. 1:44:321 Stunde, 44 Minuten, 32 SekundenNow, that study gets cited a lot and it deserves to be, but it's worth noting what it actually shows and what it doesn't. 1:44:421 Stunde, 44 Minuten, 42 SekundenIt shows that intensive sustained learning can produce measurable structural changes in the adult brain. 1:44:501 Stunde, 44 Minuten, 50 SekundenIt does not show that any casual mental activity reshapes your neurons. The taxi 1:44:561 Stunde, 44 Minuten, 56 Sekundendrivers weren't doing crossword puzzles for 15 minutes a day. They were engaged in years of effortful, highstakes 1:45:061 Stunde, 45 Minuten, 6 Sekundenspatial learning. Exactly the kind of deep construction we've been talking about all evening. The change was real, but it was earned. 1:45:171 Stunde, 45 Minuten, 17 SekundenAnd this connects back to something important about how plasticity works across the lifespan. 1:45:231 Stunde, 45 Minuten, 23 SekundenIn childhood, the brain is in a period of exuberant overprouction. 1:45:291 Stunde, 45 Minuten, 29 SekundenYou're born with roughly the number of neurons you'll ever have, give or take some exceptions we'll get to, but the 1:45:361 Stunde, 45 Minuten, 36 Sekundenconnections between them multiply wildly in the first few years of life. A 2-year-old's brain has roughly twice as many synaptic connections as an adult. 1:45:481 Stunde, 45 Minuten, 48 SekundenThat sounds like an advantage, and in some ways it is, because all that excess connectivity is what makes young children such flexible learners. 1:45:591 Stunde, 45 Minuten, 59 SekundenBut the real work of development isn't just building connections. It's pruning them. The brain figures out which 1:46:081 Stunde, 46 Minuten, 8 Sekundenconnections are useful by seeing which ones get used. And the ones that don't get reinforced gradually weaken and disappear. 1:46:181 Stunde, 46 Minuten, 18 SekundenThis is sometimes described as use it or lose it, which is a bit of a simplification, but the basic principle holds. 1:46:281 Stunde, 46 Minuten, 28 SekundenExperience sculpts the brain by deciding which of the many possible circuits actually survive. In adulthood, that 1:46:361 Stunde, 46 Minuten, 36 Sekundenprocess is different. You're not starting from a state of wild overconnection. 1:46:421 Stunde, 46 Minuten, 42 SekundenInstead, the brain modifies existing circuits, strengthens some, weakens others, and occasionally in certain 1:46:511 Stunde, 46 Minuten, 51 Sekundenregions grows new ones. For decades, it was believed that adult neurogenesis, the birth of entirely new neurons, simply didn't happen. 1:47:021 Stunde, 47 Minuten, 2 SekundenBut work by researchers like Fred Gage at the Sulkq Institute in the late 1990s demonstrated that new neurons do form in 1:47:101 Stunde, 47 Minuten, 10 Sekundenthe adult hippocampus, at least in some species. And there's evidence, though it remains debated, that this occurs in 1:47:181 Stunde, 47 Minuten, 18 Sekundenhumans, too. A study led by Kirsty Spalding in 2013 used an ingenious 1:47:251 Stunde, 47 Minuten, 25 Sekundenmethod involving carbon 14 from cold war nuclear testing to date the age of neurons in human brains and found 1:47:341 Stunde, 47 Minuten, 34 Sekundenevidence consistent with ongoing neurogenesis in the hippocampus. 1:47:391 Stunde, 47 Minuten, 39 SekundenBut the field is genuinely unsettled on this. A prominent 2018 study by Sorrels 1:47:471 Stunde, 47 Minuten, 47 Sekundenand colleagues found very little evidence of new neuron formation in the adult human hippocampus. And the back 1:47:541 Stunde, 47 Minuten, 54 Sekundenand forth continues. The honest answer is that we're not sure how much adult neurogenesis matters in humans. And 1:48:031 Stunde, 48 Minuten, 3 Sekundenanyone who tells you confidently that you're growing new brain cells every time you learn something is getting ahead of the evidence. 1:48:121 Stunde, 48 Minuten, 12 SekundenWhat is well established though is synaptic plasticity, the strengthening and weakening of existing connections. 1:48:201 Stunde, 48 Minuten, 20 SekundenAnd this is where the mechanism gets genuinely beautiful in a quiet molecular sort of way. 1:48:281 Stunde, 48 Minuten, 28 SekundenWhen you learn something, when you really engage with it and construct understanding rather than just letting 1:48:351 Stunde, 48 Minuten, 35 Sekundeninformation wash over you, a cascade of events occurs at the synaptic level. The neurotransmitter glutamate is released. 1:48:441 Stunde, 48 Minuten, 44 SekundenReceptors on the receiving neuron respond. If the signal is strong enough and repeated enough, a process called 1:48:521 Stunde, 48 Minuten, 52 Sekundenlong-term potentiation kicks in, which is essentially the syninnapse becoming more efficient at transmitting that 1:49:001 Stunde, 49 Minutenparticular signal in the future. The connection gets easier to activate. And if you keep using it, structural changes 1:49:091 Stunde, 49 Minuten, 9 Sekundenfollow. New receptor sites appear. The syninnapse physically grows. The pathway 1:49:161 Stunde, 49 Minuten, 16 Sekundenbecomes, in a very literal sense, a well-worn trail. And here's what's beautiful about this from the 1:49:241 Stunde, 49 Minuten, 24 Sekundenperspective of everything we've been talking about tonight. 1:49:281 Stunde, 49 Minuten, 28 SekundenLong-term potentiation is not triggered by passive exposure. It requires active repeated engagement. 1:49:371 Stunde, 49 Minuten, 37 SekundenIt requires the kind of effortful retrieval that Bjork's desirable difficulties describe. It requires the 1:49:451 Stunde, 49 Minuten, 45 Sekundenkind of construction that the constructivists insist on. It requires the kind of sleep consolidation that 1:49:521 Stunde, 49 Minuten, 52 Sekundenstick gold and walker documented where the brain replays and strengthens the day's learning during slowwave sleep and REM. 1:50:021 Stunde, 50 Minuten, 2 SekundenAll the different threads we've followed tonight, from Ebingous's forgetting curves to Pia's disequilibrium to the 1:50:091 Stunde, 50 Minuten, 9 Sekundentesting effect to Vigotssky's zone of proximal development, they all converge on the same physical reality. 1:50:181 Stunde, 50 Minuten, 18 SekundenLearning is the brain remodeling itself in response to experience. And the remodeling is deeper and more lasting 1:50:271 Stunde, 50 Minuten, 27 Sekundenwhen the experience is effortful, spaced, and actively constructed rather than passively received. 1:50:361 Stunde, 50 Minuten, 36 SekundenWhich means that everything your brain has been doing tonight as you've been lying here listening has been a kind of gentle construction. 1:50:461 Stunde, 50 Minuten, 46 SekundenNot the intense, effortful kind that reshapes your hippocampus over 4 years of memorizing London streets, but something quieter. 1:50:561 Stunde, 50 Minuten, 56 SekundenYou've been hearing ideas, connecting them to things you already knew, noticing when something surprised you or didn't quite fit with your expectations. 1:51:071 Stunde, 51 Minuten, 7 SekundenThat slight friction, that moment of, "Wait, really?" when you heard that children's brains have twice as many 1:51:151 Stunde, 51 Minuten, 15 Sekundenconnections as adults, or that the most famous memory patient in history couldn't form new memories, but could still learn to trace a star. That 1:51:241 Stunde, 51 Minuten, 24 Sekundenfriction is the feeling of your existing mental models adjusting. 1:51:291 Stunde, 51 Minuten, 29 SekundenIt's PJ's accommodation happening in real time so gently you might not have noticed. And some of what you heard tonight will fade. 1:51:391 Stunde, 51 Minuten, 39 SekundenThat's not a failure. That's Ebingous's curve doing exactly what it does. And it's fine. 1:51:461 Stunde, 51 Minuten, 46 SekundenThe things that stick will be the things that connected to something you already cared about or surprised you enough to 1:51:541 Stunde, 51 Minuten, 54 Sekundencreate a little disequilibrium or that you'll encounter again somewhere else in the next few days and think, 1:52:011 Stunde, 52 Minuten, 1 Sekunde"Oh, right. I heard something about that." That second encounter, that accidental spacing is one of the most 1:52:101 Stunde, 52 Minuten, 10 Sekundenpowerful things that can happen to a memory. 1:52:141 Stunde, 52 Minuten, 14 SekundenNot because you're trying to remember, just because the world brought it back and your brain recognized the pattern. 1:52:231 Stunde, 52 Minuten, 23 SekundenThe thing that researchers keep coming back to across all the different traditions and frameworks and decades of argument is that learning is not a thing 1:52:321 Stunde, 52 Minuten, 32 Sekundenthat happens to you. It's a thing you do. 1:52:371 Stunde, 52 Minuten, 37 SekundenSometimes deliberately, sometimes without realizing it, sometimes in your sleep. 1:52:441 Stunde, 52 Minuten, 44 SekundenYour brain has been doing it since before you were born, when you were floating in the dark, learning the 1:52:511 Stunde, 52 Minuten, 51 Sekundenrhythm of your mother's voice, and it will keep doing it for as long as you're alive. The machinery slows down. Yes, 1:53:001 Stunde, 53 Minutenthe wild plasticity of childhood gives way to something more targeted, more deliberate, more dependent on effort and repetition. 1:53:101 Stunde, 53 Minuten, 10 SekundenBut it never stops. The brain never stops being willing to change if you give it something worth changing for. 1:53:181 Stunde, 53 Minuten, 18 SekundenAnd right now, the part of your brain that consolidates the day's learning into longerterm storage is beginning to 1:53:251 Stunde, 53 Minuten, 25 Sekundendo its work. The hippocampus, that seahorse-shaped structure that featured so prominently in Henry Malay's story, 1:53:341 Stunde, 53 Minuten, 34 Sekundenis preparing to replay fragments of today's experience, strengthening some connections, letting others fade, filing 1:53:431 Stunde, 53 Minuten, 43 Sekundenthings in places you won't consciously know about until something triggers them later. You don't have to do anything for this to happen. You don't have to try. 1:53:541 Stunde, 53 Minuten, 54 SekundenThe system knows what it's doing. It's been doing it every night of your life and it's very very good at it. So 1:54:031 Stunde, 54 Minuten, 3 Sekundenwhatever you're carrying from today, whatever you learned or struggled with or half understood, your brain will take care of it. It will 1:54:131 Stunde, 54 Minuten, 13 Sekundensort through the noise and find the signal. It will keep the things that matter and let the rest go quietly. 1:54:211 Stunde, 54 Minuten, 21 SekundenAnd tomorrow or next week or in some idle moment months from now, something will surface that you didn't know you'd 1:54:301 Stunde, 54 Minuten, 30 Sekundenkept. A connection you didn't know you'd made. An understanding that assembled itself while you weren't looking. 1:54:391 Stunde, 54 Minuten, 39 SekundenThat's the deepest thing about learning. 1:54:421 Stunde, 54 Minuten, 42 SekundenThe part that none of the theories quite capture. 1:54:461 Stunde, 54 Minuten, 46 SekundenSo much of it happens in the dark, in the quiet, in the spaces between effort. 1:54:531 Stunde, 54 Minuten, 53 SekundenThe mind builds while you rest. It always has. And it's building now, steadily, patiently on your side, asking 1:55:021 Stunde, 55 Minuten, 2 Sekundennothing of you but Alle Von Cosmo Explains Psychology Lerninhalte Ähnlich Empfehlungen Kürzlich hochgeladen Angesehen 2:06:41 Neu Literally Everything About How Data Centers Work Explained Slowly (For Sleep) Cosmo Explains und 3 weitere 205 vor 2 Std. 1:38:37 Neu Nobody Can Upset You When You Stop Doing These 3 Things | Audiobook Legacy Voices Audiobook 23 vor 10 Std. 1:00:50 Neu Stop Overthinking Every Problem — Train Your Mind to Find Solutions | Audiobook Better Mind Audiobook 130 vor 9 Std. 1:51:39 Neu Everything About the Psychology of Creativity Explained Slowly (For Sleep) Cosmo Explains Psychology 1631 vor 1 Tag 1:35:38 Neu Six AIs brainstorm new content - What AI Thinks - ep022 A Podcast Run by AI 1354 vor 10 Std. 22:10 Neu Psychologie Der Verbalen Intelligenz: Wie Du Deine Gedanken Richtig Formulierst Psychskizzen 7445 vor 4 Tagen 1:33:29 Neu Was Würde Tatsächlich Passieren, Wenn Du Mit Lichtgeschwindigkeit Reisen Würdest | 4K | Doku Späte Wissenschaft 827 vor 6 Std. 1:57:23 Melodic House & Techno Sessions #009 | 2-Hour Mix Atrynox Music 27 vor 5 Monaten 1:45:42 Neu Wir empfangen immer wieder rätselhafte Signale, die wir nicht erklären können Aveline Iroah 972 vor 3 Tagen 1:51:49 Neu Everything About How the Inner Voice Works Explained Slowly (For Sleep) Cosmo Explains Psychology 17.619 vor 4 Tagen 1:29:29 Solitary - Gefangen im All (Sci-Fi | 2020 | packender SPACE Thriller | Deutsch) Utopja - Sci-Fi Filme 198.990 vor 1 Jahr 1:07:07 Dark Rave 2026 🖤 What the Darkness Left Behind · Peak Time Dark Techno Session KRYA Dark Tech 139.200 vor 5 Monaten 57:55 Melodic House & Techno Sessions #010 | Syn